      <h1>Keeping API Mocks Honest with Pact Contract Testing</h1>

      <div class="not-prose my-8 rounded-lg border border-indigo-200 bg-indigo-50/60 p-6 dark:border-indigo-500/30 dark:bg-indigo-500/5">
        <p class="mb-0 text-indigo-900/90 dark:text-indigo-200/90"><strong>Pact contract testing catches the moment your frontend's API mocks stop matching the real API, before it reaches production.</strong> Your consumer's tests record the requests they make and the responses they rely on into a contract (a "pact"). The provider's CI replays that contract against the real service. This guide builds a complete TypeScript example, shows real failure output, and connects Pact to the MSW mocks your frontend already uses.</p>
      </div>

      <blockquote>
        <p><strong>Tested with</strong> @pact-foundation/pact 17.1.4 (Pact Specification V4), Vitest 5.0.5, MSW 3.0.3 and Express 5.3.0 on Node 22.23.3 (2026-10-15). The consumer test, provider verification, failure output and MSW-from-pact helper all ran on our machine. We didn't run a Pact Broker or PactFlow: those commands come from the official docs and CLI help. devxtools.dev is independent and has no relationship with the Pact Foundation or SmartBear (PactFlow).</p>
      </blockquote>

      <h2 id="tldr">TL;DR</h2>

      <p>Pact is a consumer-driven contract testing tool. Your consumer's tests record the requests and responses they rely on into a contract, and the provider replays that contract against the real service to prove it still matches. Use it alongside MSW or Prism mocks, so the mocks your frontend relies on can't silently drift from the API.</p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
